ing and uh you know he  says in the question that they've made  their hou the house their home it's not  their home it's your home it'll never be  their home as long as they're renting it  good point and when you're a renter you  kind of know that yeah i've been a  renter and i i didn't expect to stay  there  i'm passing through you know no one that  rents very few people that rent go i  want to be here 50 years i think i'm  just going to stay in your house for 50  years  you know no one does that this is my  forever home i'm a renter no one says  this  and honestly people don't say you know i  like taking it situation by situation  and going is this a true hardship or is  it really you've got to tighten up the  budget and so at that point i may give  them some resources maybe some ramsey  resources say hey here's the total money  makeover i want to help you create that  margin i'd love to keep you as a tenant  you've been great  and how you interact with them in  kindness is fine too you don't have to  be unkind you don't have to be cold and  transactional you can go understand it  hurts my heart  but i'm doing this with all these things  now if you've got a property that's gone  way up maybe you stage it up  and that's how you'd want to be treated  if you were there treat other people  like you won't be treated but if you  really would expect to get to live there  on rental rates that are 75 percent of  the market forever just because your  landlord's a christian then you've got  something screwed in your brain  you really do that's just not how it's  not that is not a calling of the bible  that is not something scripture calls us  to  to under charge because we're christians  now we don't need to overcharge we don't  need to rip people off we certainly want  to tell the truth  and those kinds of things we're not  trying to take advantage in some way  but sometimes people get in their head  that anybody that sells anything can't  be a christian  you know i can't b